    "result_text": "All 6 tests pass. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## The rule\n\nA new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at **one above the highest membership level currently held by an existing member of their household** (owners sharing normalized last name + postcode). With no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n## Changes\n\nSince `membershipLevel` is a *derived* value (computed by `OwnerMapper` \u2192 `MembershipLevel.of`, never stored), and the test reads the value back via `GET`, the ceiling has to be fixed at creation and persisted so later reads reflect it \u2014 mirroring how `householdMemberCount`/`namesakeCount` already work.\n\n- **`CapMembershipLevel`** (new pipeline function) \u2014 iterates existing active owners, takes the max `MembershipLevel.of` among same-household members, and stores `max + 1` on the owner. Leaves the cap null when there are no existing members. Reuses `Households.sameHousehold`, `OwnerRepository.findAllActive`, and `MembershipLevel`.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the `capMembershipLevel` step in after `countHousehold`, under `transaction` governance like its neighbours.\n- **`MembershipLevel.of(owner, asOf)`** \u2014 applies `min(level, cap)` when a cap is present. This keeps all level semantics in the one class that owns them; `points`/`level` are untouched, so `membershipPoints` stays uncapped as the spec intends.\n- **`Owner`** \u2014 new nullable `membershipLevelCap` field/column (+ `toString`).\n- **Schema** \u2014 `membership_level_cap INTEGER` added to all four DBs (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres).\n- **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 ignore the new target in both `toOwner` overloads (consistent with `householdMemberCount`).\n\n## One test adjustment (worth flagging)\n\nThe provided test created a second owner in the *same* household but omitted `sharesHousehold`, so it was rejected with **409** by the pre-existing household-uniqueness rule (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`) \u2014 before the cap logic could ever run. \"Household members\" only exist through that documented opt-in, which is exactly what the cap operates on. I added `b.put(\"sharesHousehold\", true)` so B is a valid declared second member; the cap assertion is unchanged. I chose this over weakening the duplicate-block rule, which has no basis in the spec.\n\nNote the given scenario passes even without capping (B naturally lands at level 2, cap also 2) \u2014 but the implementation correctly enforces the ceiling whenever an owner's raw level would otherwise exceed it.",
